<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aratus of Sicyon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">271–<a href="/years/213-bce">213 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Aratos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aratus of Sicyon was a Greek statesman who freed his city from its tyrant in 251 BCE, brought it into the Achaean League and led the league for over thirty years as its general. He seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> from its Macedonian garrison in 243 BCE and expanded the league across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, but when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> defeated him he called in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a>, reversing his anti-Macedonian policy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon.jpg" alt="Aratus of Sicyon" width="320" height="322" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aratus_of_Sicyon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aratus was born in 271 BCE in Sicyon in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. His father Cleinias had become head magistrate of the city after the murder of the previous tyrant. In 264 BCE Abantidas murdered Cleinias and made himself tyrant, and the seven-year-old Aratus escaped with the help of his aunt, who hid him until nightfall and smuggled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>. He was raised there by friends of his father and won a prize in the pentathlon, but remained set on freeing Sicyon, and became leader of the exiles' party. He sought support from the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 251 BCE, acting on a prisoner's tip that the escape route out of Sicyon could be used to scale its walls, Aratus led a band of exiles in with ladders at night. The tyrant Nicocles escaped through a tunnel, and some citizens burned his house. Aratus recalled the exiles and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, brought Sicyon into the Achaean League, the first non-Achaean city admitted.</p><p>He travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and returned with 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> and a promise of 110 more from Ptolemy II Philadelphos, which helped settle disputes over confiscated property. Elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of the League in 245 BCE, he held the office every two years with few interruptions, seventeen times in all.</p><p>In 243 BCE he took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> from its Macedonian garrison under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persaeus">Persaeus</a>, using a less steep approach learned from Syrian mercenaries. Corinth, Megara, Troezen and Epidaurus then joined the League. After the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and Corinth, Aratus secured an alliance with his former enemy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and their forces defeated Cleomenes at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sellasia">Battle of Sellasia</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-220-217-bce">Social War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a> he became a chief adviser to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a>. He was weaker as a battlefield commander: at the river Xerias near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> in 235 BCE he retreated while the other wing was winning, and in 220 BCE a cavalry action he ordered against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolians">Aetolians</a> ended in a rout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Aratus quarrelled with Philip V over his conduct at Messene, and he died in 213 BCE. Plutarch reports that Philip had him poisoned, but modern scholarship finds this unlikely, since Philip had no interest in an unstable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. By special permission of the Oracle of Delphi he was the first person buried within the walls of Sicyon, as founder and saviour of the city. Plutarch judged him a great politician whose abilities did not extend to the battlefield. He was skilled at compromise, as when he handed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> in return for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and under him the League became a major power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aratus of Sicyon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The decisive expansion beyond Achaea began in 251 BCE, when the young <b>Aratus</b> overthrew the tyrant Nicocles of Sicyon and brought his city, which was not Achaean, into the league.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through Polybius, Livy and Plutarch's lives of Demetrius, <b>Aratus</b> and Aemilius Paullus, the dynasty became for later readers a classic example of Hellenistic kingship and of its defeat by Rome.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>From 251 BCE, when <b>Aratus of Sicyon</b> freed his city and joined it to the Achaean League, the league drove them out or persuaded them to step down: Lydiadas of Megalopolis laid down his tyranny in 235 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/government/tyranny">Tyranny · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Macedonian kings garrisoned Acrocorinth until <b>Aratus</b> took it for the Achaean League in 243 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li><li><blockquote>In 241 BCE the Aetolian League sacked it, and <b>Aratus</b> routed the Aetolians in its streets.</blockquote><a href="/state/pellene">Pellene</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aratus</b> freed Sicyon from tyranny in 251 BCE and joined it to the Achaean League, which he then led.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicyon">Sicyon</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sicyon, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,22.72&amp;year=-234" aria-label="Sicyon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5116 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aratus of Sicyon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5566 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5566 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,22.72&amp;year=-234">What was here in 234 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
